n on the other  you have fantasy well that's our dream  life that's the stories that we tell  each other every day there was always a  good reason for science fiction and  fantasy to be central but there was a  short period of time where you had to be  doing what I call domestic realism to be  legitimate in the modernist system but I  will say the literature department here  at UCSD all the way back to 1970 which  is when I first encountered it was  always extremely welcoming to science  fiction crazies like myself mainly  because of the professors that I met and  so it was a matter of accident and  that's that's a good thing about UCSD  and maybe why UCSD has had so many great  science fiction writers come out of it  not out of the literature department but  at least out of UCSD yeah it should be  you know the division between high  literature and low literature culture is  actually a relatively recent thing in in  global history you know if you if you go  back to you know the Elizabethan theatre  Shakespeare was Shakespeare was not some  Queenie Twitty guy he was writing for  the ground like this what does anything  else he was including plenty of rival  jokes and sword fights and you know  exciting stuff in his plays that would  please the people who were just standing  in the pit and eating an onion because  they didn't they might throw that onion  and they were very conscious of audience  reaction I think a lot of the division  you know that produced this attitude  dates from Henry James and Robert Louis  Stevenson most of you know that story  from your lip courses I don't want to  bore you if you already do but I don't  think they know it again okay Henry  James had love for the Stevenson had a  celebrated literary quarrel at one point  which was brought on by James writing  the review of two books one of which was  Treasure Island by Stevenson and the  other which was a book about a young boy  I don't remember the author it was a  book about a young boy and provincial  France coming of age growing up and  James in this review contrasted to two  books and he  for me the pleasure of literature is  seeing the author's depiction of the  world and the recognition it brings in  me and I I go through it and I say yes  he got that right no I don't think he's  describing that right you know the  quality that ingredients to comment  shared experiences of people in the real  world experiencing real things and in  the case of these two books he said well  the the Treasure Island is far more  accomplished the prose is better it's  it's altogether better written but it's  it's very unrealistic and he said  differential book despite all of its  flaws it's about a child growing up and  that's an experience I have had I I have  been a small child trying to come to  terms with adulthood so I have been a  child but I have never been a pirate  hunting for buried gold and having sword  fights and dealing with the black spot  and get a yachtie and so therefore even  though Treasure Island is more  accomplished book the French book is a  more worthy book it's a more serious  book about a serious subject and the  other one is just sort of fun Stevenson  responded in the next issue the magazine  with a you know a rather famous  one-sentence thing here you know mr.  James says that he has been a he has  been a child but he has never been a  pirate company from very gold then  obviously he has never been a child and  James responded to that and and many  other people got involved and it became  a very long literary debate which the  academic and literary establishment of  the time subsequently decided in James  had won and from that point on  Stephenson and all of the Treasure  Island's and kidnaps and dr. Jekyll and  mr. Hyde and ELISA us were sort of  consigned to Jean or lettuce or Kitty  literature fun for children but not  about serious subjects in the real world  if you want to be a serious writer you  have to write about you know  coming-o